close encounters of the huh kind horses  in the night skies cowboys and aliens  and predators all feature in jordan  peel's newest film nope and you don't  need access to any x-files to understand  it all keep watching for the confusing  moments in nope explained  early in the film nope introduces oj  haywood and his father otis senior the  two run a horse ranch with a side  business of wrangling the animals for  hollywood films and their intro scene  starts off pretty quiet and peaceful  that is until an array of objects start  raining from the sky like daggers one of  which fatally wounds otis the scene is  pretty clearly explained via the later  events in the film but it can be a bit  confusing at first while oj is told by  the authorities that the items likely  fell out of an airplane he doesn't buy  that explanation eventually he discovers  that a giant alien monster is to blame a  creature that vomits out any non-organic  material after feeding so presumably the  alien was passing overhead after a meal  cloaked in its cloud cover and  unfortunately decided to spit up some  keys and coins directly over haywood  ranch  following the brutal opening combo of  gordy's home and otis death knope  launches into a full opening credits  sequence something that's seen less and  less in modern movies but common to the  classic genre flicks that peel pulls  from the only thing is it's hard to tell  exactly what's going on during the  opening credits the names appear in the  middle of an oscillating landscape of  waves almost like the viewer is sitting  inside an otherworldly blanket fort  eventually the camera zooms in to reveal  a man riding a horse a clip later  explained to be the first movie ever  made it's impossible to discern what's  happening your first time watching the  nope opening credits but after going  through the full film it becomes much  clearer the camera shot that displays  the credits is the same shot used to  show the inside of the alien's mouth  later on the flags from the decoy are  repeatedly shown dangling out the  billowing maw and the shot is used again  in the star lasso experience scene when  the creature devours a whole show's  worth of people this could just be a fun  easter egg for folks on their second  viewing but it can also be read as  thematically significant ricky refers to  the ufo as the viewers and that's also  what you are when you're watching the  movie rattling off the cast and crew  from the perspective of the monster  makes that connection even more explicit  and the texture of the alien also  foreshadows its final form  after getting kicked off the set of a  commercial oj and emerald head to the  wild west theme park jupiter's claim  owned by the enigmatic ricky park  specifically oj goes there to sell  another horse lucky to ricky having  already sold him almost a dozen while in  the meeting o.j brings up the notion of  buying a few of the horses back which  causes ricky to pause and eventually  change the subject this scene makes a  lot more sense with the context of the  rest of the movie it's implied that  ricky has been experimenting with the  alien for some time by feeding it horses  a grim act that eventually becomes the  centerpiece of his star lasso experience  ricky probably balks at the idea of  selling oj the horses back because well  they're not around anymore instead of  discussing the issue ricky switches  gears and tells emerald all about his  experience shooting the sitcom gordy's  home the site of the violent incident  pictured in the first scene this moment  also foreshadows the star lasso  experience as it shows ricky's ability  to ignore trauma and potential danger in  the interest of turning a profit  after setting up security cameras all  around the ranch and preparing for the  ufo's return oj has what seems at first  to be a real alien encounter in the  horse's stables while investigating some  strange activity he comes upon three  big-headed beings covered in coarse  black hair it's one of the film's  spookier scenes but it ends up being a  complete fake out as the aliens are  nothing more than three kids in costumes  specifically they're the children of  ricky park showing up later on in the  same costumes to perform at the star  lasso experience after their cover is  blown they tell oj not to mess with  jupiter's claim and quickly leave oj  seems to think it's because emerald  stole one of the park's horse statues  but there might be more at play than  simple payback it's suggested at various  points that ricky is interested in  purchasing the whole haywood ranch given  that his newest business venture  involves chasing down a ufo that lives  in the area that makes a lot of sense  considering that it's possible the kid's  prank would have been just the start of  a campaign to compel oj to sell  businessmen using skeevy tactics to  force people to sell property is a  classic western trope and it feels right  at home in nope  arguably the most graphic and striking  scene in all of nope happens after the  alien eats the people of the star lasso  experience the creature descends  directly over the haywood house and  begins releasing all manner of objects  coins keys and a giant statue of a horse  all get jettisoned out of the creature's  maw but it also spews a veritable  hailstorm of blood and viscera from its  recently consumed meal in other words  the alien starts raining literal blood  over the haywood house  [Applause]  the spark for this horrifying moment is  the aliens feast at jupiter's claim it  sucks up way more people and other  assorted items than ever before so it  makes sense that its release ends up  being so massive wyatt chooses to dump  all over the haywood house specifically  however is a bit less clear the best  explanation is probably oj's own it's a  territorial animal and it's marking its  territory  despite being one of the two lead  protagonists of nope o.j haywood doesn't  get a ton of overt character development  he's a quiet and solitary man who seems  to prefer the company of horses to that  of humans but he also doesn't seem to  love the hollywood work that his father  pushed him into even ufo attacks and  reigning blood barely get a rise out of  oj who willingly returns to the ranch  time after time despite the inherent  danger  no  why does oj do this knowing that he  could get abducted and devoured you  could read it as oj wanting the money  emerald claims that a great shot of the  alien will make them rich and famous  which would definitely be a motivating  factor for most people but is it the  kind of thing that oj would care about  as oj tells emerald before returning to  the ranch he's got mouths to feed he  clearly feels a familial obligation to  take care of the horses and keep the  ranch going he stays in the hollywood  business in large part because it's what  otis would have wanted and he may see  exposing the alien as a way of paying  tribute to his father as well if there  is money to be made from the alien it  would mean that oj wouldn't need to sell  any more horses which seems good enough  for him  the climax of nope is a complicated  sequence and there are a lot of things  that are easy to miss your first time  watching it essentially the whole group  lays out an elaborate plan to capture  the alien fully on camera each character  has their own motivations with angel  wanting to expose extraterrestrials to  the public emerald seeking out the fame  and fortune a great shot could bring and  holst seeking out some kind of artistic  achievement by making an impossible shot  the shot the money shot undeniable  singular  the oprah shot  one of the more confusing parts of the  plan involves the dozens of skydancers  that the crew sets up all around the  ranch at first they look like they're  meant as distractions but they're  actually a sort of radar system because  anything electrical conks out when near  the alien you can tell where the  creature is by which skydancers get  turned off the electrical issues are  also why holst brings an old-fashioned  hand crank camera which doesn't rely on  any modern power source to operate  finally oj uses a parachute-laden string  of flags the same kind that temporarily  incapacitates the alien earlier in the  movie as a means to briefly scare it off  once the ufo gets on him and he rides  into position for the shot he releases  the string into the air to dissuade the  alien from eating him and lucky the plan  works and both oj and lucky make it out  in one piece  for his climactic ride on lucky oj  chooses to wear the orange crew hoodie  he got from working on the set of the  scorpion king with his father he  mentions the film much earlier during a  conversation with emerald who shares  that jean jacket the horse that was  supposed to be her first was the one  brought along for the shoot the scorpion  king generally seems to symbolize the  complicated relationship that the  haywood siblings had with their father  it was the first job that oj got brought  along for it marked a schism between  otis and emerald and because jean jacket  didn't even end up being used in the  film it also represents unfinished  business he even goes so far as to dub  the alien jean jacket  all of these little details surely play  into oj's decision to wear the hoodie he  brings in all these loose ends from his  relationship with his father and it  seems like he gets some closure by  confronting the alien of course the  hoodie is functionally relevant as its  bright orange color is easy for both the  alien and the rest of the crew to see  and it's big enough for oj to wrap  himself in what looks to be a  bulletproof vest for extra protection  emerald even sews some i-like reflectors  into the back of the hood to help get  the creature's attention when the time  comes  just as everything is set for the big  alien shoot the crew gets disrupted by  the surprise arrival of a paparazzi  reporter riding in on an electric  motorcycle with a striking chrome helmet  that completely obscures his face the  reporter quickly begins badgering  emerald with questions about the strange  incidents in the area because he looks  like a robot from the future it's  natural to assume at first that the  motorcycle man has some ulterior motive  for being there but in the end he just  dies in a relatively unceremonious  fashion the main reasons for him showing  up seem to be getting emerald a  motorcycle something she references  early on and offering another bit of  meta commentary like holst the reporter  is there to get the shot even as he's  lying on the ground about to die he  implores oj to take a picture of him  nope dabbles heavily in the idea of  seeing and being seen on camera or  otherwise for a guy so obsessed with  being seen and getting the perfect shot  it's more than a little ironic that he  was eaten by a creature that definitely  saw him  shockingly the whole plan to capture  footage of the ufo goes perfectly at  least until host decides to take a  camera with him up a hill where he's  quickly eaten by the monster holst is a  curious character throughout the film to  say the least whenever he's shown  editing film it's mostly nature shots of  predators fighting he agrees to help out  when emerald promises an opportunity to  get the impossible shot which suggests  that he's still chasing some kind of  artistic peak and yet once he has the  shot holst seems to lose all sense of  himself we don't deserve the impossible  he says before climbing up the hill with  his camera this seems like a declaration  of intent holst willingly travels to  higher and more dangerous ground with  the camera in tow in an attempt to make  the shot even more perfect and what's  more perfect than what he already has  more of the alien at golden hour he  films the aliens approach and attack  even as his camera is destroyed along  with him he gets the shot at the  ultimate price check 